fn async_write < 'a >(& 'a mut self , buf : & 'a [ u8 ]) -> Result < AsyncStatus >; }

pub trait AsyncRead {
    /// Attempts to read enough bytes to fill the provided buffer.
    fn async_read < 'a >(& 'a mut self , buf : & 'a mut [ u8 ]) -> Result < AsyncStatus >;
}

Notice that the write and read functions now return a type called AsyncStatus instead of just a simple Result. This AsyncStatus type can represent either a file descriptor or a timeout, depending on the specific operation being performed. Using this AsyncStatus type, we can now perform asynchronous I/O operations.

To see this in action, let's imagine writing some code that asynchronously reads from a file:

let mut file = File :: open ( "my_file.txt" ). unwrap ();
let mut buffer = [ 0 ; CHUNK_SIZE ];
loop {
    match file . async_read ( & mut buffer ) {
        Ok ( AsyncStatus :: Fd ( _ )) => {
            // We got some data from the file
            // Do something with the data
        },
        Ok ( AsyncStatus :: Timeout ( _ )) => {
            // We timed out before getting any data
            // Do something else
        },
        Err ( e ) => {
            // There was an error
            if e . kind () == ErrorKind :: WouldBlock {
                // This just means we need to wait for more data
                continue ;
            } else {
                // Some other kind of error occurred
                panic !( "Error reading file: {}" , e );
            }
        }
    }
}

This code uses the async_read function to read data from a file. If the read operation succeeds, it returns an AsyncStatus::Fd value indicating that we got some data. If the read operation times out, it returns an AsyncStatus::Timeout value. If some other kind of error occurs, it simply panics.

